        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127568,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma shows a predilection for the dorsal hands and fingers, lower extremities, and trunk or back {8615046}. The head and neck are rarely involved.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127569,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma presents as solitary or multiple smooth erythematous papules and nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127570,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This relatively uncommon lesion arises in middle age. Some studies have demonstrated a female predisposition {25140659}, whereas others have shown equal incidence between sexes {30927287}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127571,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127572,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Its pathogenesis has not fully been elucidated. Several mechanisms have been hypothesized, including a role for fibroblasts, ER&alpha; overexpression, and mast cells {33180332; 20644465}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127573,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127574,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 306,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma consists of a triad of multinucleated cells, stromal fibrosis, and vascular changes. The multinucleated cells are angulated and stellate, with elongated cytoplasmic extensions. They may be few in number and revealed by deeper tissue levels {30927287}. Stromal fibrosis is often parallel to the epidermis. Increased numbers of small-calibre vessels within the papillary and reticular dermis show thickened walls with prominent endothelial cells.</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>The multinucleated cells may express CD68 (variable) and factor&nbsp;XIIIa, yet they are generally negative for CD163, CD34, and factor&nbsp;VIII {19336021; 30927287}. The vascular proliferation is highlighted by conventional vascular markers (CD31, CD34, ERG, factor&nbsp;VIII).</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>Fibrous papule (angiofibroma) of the nose is a close mimic but affects the head and neck and shows concentric perifollicular fibrosis. The atrophic pattern of dermatofibroma can resemble multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma but shows storiform rather than parallel fibrosis and favours the lower extremities. Telangiectasia macularis eruptiva perstans can also display multinucleated stromal cells but usually presents with eosinophils and increased mast cells. Multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma should be distinguished from reactive multinucleate cell angiohistiocytoma&ndash;like changes that may present as a focal histological finding or within another dermatological lesion {31070801}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:59:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
